== Solution 6 ==
<asy>
unitsize(1.2cm);

// Define vertices of the regular hexagon
pair F = (-3, 0);
pair E = (-1.5, 2.598);
pair D = (1.5, 2.598);
pair C = (3, 0);
pair B = (1.5, -2.598);
pair A = (-1.5, -2.598);

// Draw the main hexagon
draw(A--B--C--D--E--F--cycle, linewidth(1pt));

// Define the inner parallel line points
pair Y = (-2.5, 0.866);
pair X = (2.5, 0.866);
pair Z = (-2.5, -0.866);
pair W = (2.5, -0.866);

// Draw the dashed inner lines
draw(Y--X, dashed);
draw(Z--W, dashed);

// Define construction points G, H, J, K from notebook
pair G = (-2.5, 0);
pair H = (-1.5, 0);
pair J = (-1.5, -0.866);
pair K = (1.5, -0.866);

// Draw full vertical and horizontal helper lines (dotted gray)
draw(Y--Z, dotted + gray + linewidth(0.8pt)); // Full vertical line ZY
draw(E--A, dotted + gray + linewidth(0.8pt)); // Full vertical line AE
draw(F--H, dotted + gray + linewidth(0.8pt)); // Horizontal line FH through G and H
draw(B--K, dotted + gray + linewidth(0.8pt)); // Vertical line BK for the area split

// Label primary vertices
label("$E$", E, N);
label("$D$", D, N);
label("$B$", B, S);
label("$A$", A, S);
label("$F$", F + (-0.2, 0));
label("$C$", C + (0.2, 0));

// Label parallel line points
label("$Y$", Y, NW);
label("$X$", X, NE);
label("$Z$", Z, SW);
label("$W$", W, SE);

// Label your helper points G, H, J, K
label("$G$", G, SE);
label("$H$", H, NE);
label("$J$", J, NW);
label("$K$", K, NE);

label("$p$", (F+Z)/2, SW);
label("$q$", (A+B)/2, S);

shipout(bbox(5mm, invisible));
</asy>

Let <imath>FY = p</imath> and let the side length of the regular hexagon be <imath>AB = q</imath>. 

Consider the region at the vertex <imath>F</imath>. Since <imath>FY = FZ</imath>, <imath>\triangle YFZ</imath> is isosceles. Constructing the angle bisector of <imath>\angle AFE = 120^\circ</imath> gives <imath>\angle ZFG = 60^\circ</imath>. By the property of isosceles triangles, <imath>\overline{FG}</imath> is an altitude (<imath>\angle ZGF = 90^\circ</imath>). Therefore, <imath>\triangle ZGF</imath> is a <imath>30^\circ</imath>-<imath>60^\circ</imath>-<imath>90^\circ</imath> triangle with hypotenuse <imath>FZ = p</imath>. Since <imath>GZ</imath> is opposite <imath>\angle ZFG = 60^\circ</imath>, <imath>GZ = \frac{p\sqrt{3}}{2}</imath>. Since the angle bisector is also a median, <imath>YZ = p\sqrt{3}</imath>.


Since there are <imath>3</imath> equal spaces between <imath>AB</imath> and <imath>ED</imath>, the total height, <imath>AE</imath> of the hexagon is:
<cmath> \text{(1)} \quad \overline{AE} = 3 \times p\sqrt{3} = 3p\sqrt{3} </cmath>

By applying the exact same geometric logic to the main regular hexagon \(ABCDEF\) with side length \(\overline{AB} = q\), we can find its total height (the distance between parallel sides \(\overline{AB}\) and \(\overline{ED}\)). Just as we did at vertex \(F\), drawing the vertical segment \(\overline{AE}\) forms an isosceles triangle \(\triangle AFE\) with \(\overline{FA} = \overline{FE} = q\) and vertex \(\angle AFE = 120^\circ\). Bisecting this angle similarly splits \(\triangle AFE\) into two \(30^\circ\text{-}60^\circ\text{-}90^\circ\) triangles, each with a hypotenuse of \(q\) and a long leg of \(\frac{q\sqrt{3}}{2}\). Therefore, the total length of the perpendicular vertical segment is:

<cmath> \text{(2)} \quad \overline{AE} = 2 \times \frac{q\sqrt{3}}{2} = q\sqrt{3} </cmath>

Equating these two expressions:
<cmath> q\sqrt{3} = 3p\sqrt{3} \implies q = 3p </cmath>

Now, we find the total area of the regular hexagon <imath>ABCDEF</imath>:
<cmath> [ABCDEF] = \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}q^2 = \frac{3\sqrt{3}}{2}(3p)^2 = \frac{27p^2\sqrt{3}}{2} </cmath>

The area of the inner hexagon <imath>WCXYFZ</imath> can be found by subtracting the areas of the two isosceles trapezoids which are congruent by SSSS <imath>ABWZ</imath> and <imath>CXYD</imath> from the total area:
<cmath> \text{(3)} \quad [WCXYFZ] = [ABCDEF] - 2[ABWZ] </cmath>

For the trapezoid <imath>ABWZ</imath>:
* The shorter base is <imath>AB = q = 3p</imath>.
* The legs have length <imath>3p - p = 2p</imath>
* The height is the spacing between the parallel lines, which is <imath>p\sqrt{3}</imath>.
* The longer base <imath>ZW</imath> extends past <imath>AB</imath> by a distance of <imath>p</imath> on each side due to the <imath>30^\circ-60^\circ-90^\circ</imath> geometry in <imath>\triangle AJY</imath> and <imath>\triangle BKX</imath>, so <imath>WZ = q + 2p = 3p + 2p = 5p</imath>.

Using the trapezoid area formula:
<cmath> [ABWZ] = \frac{AB + WZ}{2} \times \text{height} = \frac{3p + 5p}{2} \times p\sqrt{3} = 4p^2\sqrt{3} </cmath>

Substituting this into equation <imath>(3)</imath>:
<cmath> [WCXYFZ] = \frac{27p^2\sqrt{3}}{2} - 2(4p^2\sqrt{3}) = \frac{27p^2\sqrt{3}}{2} - 8p^2\sqrt{3} = \frac{11p^2\sqrt{3}}{2} </cmath>

Finally, we find the required ratio:
<cmath> \frac{[WCXYFZ]}{[ABCDEF]} = \frac{\frac{11p^2\sqrt{3}}{2}}{\frac{27p^2\sqrt{3}}{2}} = \frac{11}{27} </cmath>


